<subsection><title>Video review questions</title>

<ol><li>
<problem>
<title><alert>Parameter range<!--regime--> for exponential growth/decay, function iteration form</alert></title>

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="x0" from="0.1" to=" 10" step=" 0.1" />
<select name="x">x y z s u v w </select>
<select name="n">n t </select>
<select name="a">a b c alpha beta gamma </select>
<math name="base" simplify="full">$a</math>
<math name="base_to_the_n" simplify="full">$base ^$n </math>
</setup>

<p>For the discrete dynamical system
<md>
  <mrow>$x _{ $n  +1}\amp = $a  $x _{ $n  }</mrow>
  <mrow>$x _{0}\amp = $x0 </mrow>
</md>
for what range of values of the parameter <m>$a </m> does the system exhibit exponential decay?</p>

<p>
<answer symbolicEquality="true" name="ans1">0</answer>
 <m>\lt $a  \lt</m> 
<answer symbolicEquality="true" name="ans2">1</answer>
</p>

<p>For what range of values of the parameter <m>$a </m> does the system exhibit exponential growth?</p>

<p><m>$a  \gt</m> 
<answer symbolicEquality="true" name="ans3">1</answer>
</p>

    <hint hide="($ans1.numSubmissions &lt; 2 and $ans2.numSubmissions &lt; 2 and $ans3.numSubmissions &lt; 2) or $ans1.creditAchieved + $ans2.creditAchieved + $ans3.creditAchieved = 3 or $ans1.numSubmissions >= 5 or $ans2.numSubmissions >= 5 or $ans3.numSubmissions >= 5">
  <p>We can answer this question by solving the system. The equation is already in function iteration form.
To go from <m>$x _{ $n  }</m> to <m>$x _{ $n +1}</m>, we must multiply by <m>$base </m>.  Since the initial condition is <m>$x _0 = $x0 </m>, and we must multiply by <m>$base </m> for a total of <m>$n </m> times to get to <m>$x _{ $n  }</m>,  the solution is
<me>$x _{ $n  } = $base_to_the_n $x0 .</me></p>
  </hint>

  <hint hide="($ans1.numSubmissions &lt; 5 and $ans2.numSubmissions &lt; 5 and $ans3.numSubmissions &lt; 5) or $ans1.creditAchieved + $ans2.creditAchieved + $ans3.creditAchieved = 3">
<p>We can answer this question by solving the system. The equation is already in function iteration form.
To go from <m>$x _{ $n  }</m> to <m>$x _{ $n +1}</m>, we must multiply by <m>$base </m>.  Since the initial condition is <m>$x _0 = $x0 </m>, and we must multiply by <m>$base </m> for a total of <m>$n </m> times to get to <m>$x _{ $n  }</m>,  the solution is
<me>$x _{ $n  } = $base_to_the_n $x0 .</me></p>

<p>For exponential decay we need
<me>0 \lt $base  \lt 1.</me>

</p>

<p>For exponential growth, we need
<me>$base  \gt 1.</me>
</p>
        
  </hint>

<solution>
<p>We can answer this question by solving the system. The equation is already in function iteration form.
To go from <m>$x _{ $n  }</m> to <m>$x _{ $n +1}</m>, we must multiply by <m>$base </m>.  Since the initial condition is <m>$x _0 = $x0 </m>, and we must multiply by <m>$base </m> for a total of <m>$n </m> times to get to <m>$x _{ $n  }</m>,  the solution is
<me>$x _{ $n  } = $base_to_the_n $x0 .</me></p>

<p>For exponential decay we need
<me>0 \lt $base  \lt 1.</me>

</p>

<p>For exponential growth, we need
<me>$base  \gt 1.</me>
</p>

</solution>
</problem>
</li>


<li>
<problem>
<title><alert>Parameter regime for exponential growth/decay, difference form</alert></title>

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="selectFromSequence" from="0.1" to=" 10" step=" 0.1" />
<select name="select">x y z s u v w </select>
<select name="select1">n t </select>
<select name="select2">a b c alpha beta gamma </select>
<math name="base" simplify="full">$select2[1] +1</math>
<math name="base_to_the_n" simplify="full">$base ^$select1[1] </math>
</setup>


<p>For the discrete dynamical system
<md>
  <mrow>
$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  +1} - $select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }\amp = $select2[1]  $select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }  
</mrow>
  <mrow>$select[1] _{0}\amp = $selectFromSequence[1] </mrow>
</md>
for what range of values of the parameter <m>$select2[1] </m> does the system exhibit exponential decay?</p>

<p>
<answer symbolicEquality="true" name="ans4">-1</answer>
 <m>\lt $select2[1]  \lt</m> 
<answer symbolicEquality="true" name="ans5">0</answer>
</p>

<p>For what range of values of the parameter <m>$select2[1] </m> does the system exhibit exponential growth?</p>

<p><m>$select2[1]  \gt</m> 
<answer symbolicEquality="true" name="ans6">0</answer>
</p>

  
    <hint hide="($ans4.numSubmissions &lt; 2 and $ans5.numSubmissions &lt; 2 and $ans6.numSubmissions &lt; 2) or $ans4.creditAchieved + $ans5.creditAchieved + $ans6.creditAchieved = 3 or $ans4.numSubmissions >= 5 or $ans5.numSubmissions >= 5 or $ans6.numSubmissions >= 5">
      
<p>We can answer this question by solving the system. 
Since the equation is in difference form, we add <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m> to both sides, rewriting the equation in function iteration form as <me>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  +1} = ($select2[1] +1)$select[1] _$select1[1] .</me>

To go from <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m> to <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1] +1}</m>, we must multiply by <m>$base </m>.  Since the initial condition is <m>$select[1] _0 = $selectFromSequence[1] </m>, and we must multiply by <m>$base </m> for a total of <m>$select1[1] </m> times to get to <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m>,  the solution is
<me>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  } = $base_to_the_n $selectFromSequence[1] .</me></p>
  </hint>

  <hint hide="($ans4.numSubmissions &lt; 5 and $ans5.numSubmissions &lt; 5 and $ans6.numSubmissions &lt; 5) or $ans4.creditAchieved + $ans5.creditAchieved + $ans6.creditAchieved = 3">
<p>We can answer this question by solving the system. 
Since the equation is in difference form, we add <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m> to both sides, rewriting the equation in function iteration form as <me>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  +1} = ($select2[1] +1)$select[1] _$select1[1] .</me>

To go from <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m> to <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1] +1}</m>, we must multiply by <m>$base </m>.  Since the initial condition is <m>$select[1] _0 = $selectFromSequence[1] </m>, and we must multiply by <m>$base </m> for a total of <m>$select1[1] </m> times to get to <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m>,  the solution is
<me>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  } = $base_to_the_n $selectFromSequence[1] .</me></p>

<p>For exponential decay we need
<me>0 \lt $base  \lt 1.</me>
This condition is the same as <me>-1 \lt $select2[1]  \lt 0.</me>
</p>

<p>For exponential growth, we need
<me>$base  \gt 1.</me>
This condition is the same as <me>$select2[1] > 0.</me>
</p>
  </hint>

<solution>
<p>We can answer this question by solving the system. 
Since the equation is in difference form, we add <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m> to both sides, rewriting the equation in function iteration form as <me>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  +1} = ($select2[1] +1)$select[1] _$select1[1] .</me>

To go from <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m> to <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1] +1}</m>, we must multiply by <m>$base </m>.  Since the initial condition is <m>$select[1] _0 = $selectFromSequence[1] </m>, and we must multiply by <m>$base </m> for a total of <m>$select1[1] </m> times to get to <m>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  }</m>,  the solution is
<me>$select[1] _{ $select1[1]  } = $base_to_the_n $selectFromSequence[1] .</me></p>

<p>For exponential decay we need
<me>0 \lt $base  \lt 1.</me>
This condition is the same as <me>-1 \lt $select2[1]  \lt 0.</me>
</p>

<p>For exponential growth, we need
<me>$base  \gt 1.</me>
This condition is the same as <me>$select2[1] > 0.</me>
</p>
</solution>

</problem>
</li>
</ol>
</subsection>

</section>


